--TEST--
Core\Random::sample answers in an order that is itself drawn rather than the subject's, which is where PHP's array_rand is diverged from — spec § 11, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// PHP's `array_rand` hands its picks back in the *subject's* order, which
// quietly leaks that order into every sample; `nvs_stdlib::random::sample`'s
// doc comment owns the divergence and the reason — a draw's order is drawn
// too. Nothing observes that by counting entries, so the sibling case
// (`random-draws-over-an-array`) cannot see it and an implementation that
// preserved the subject's order passes every line there. This case asks about
// the order alone, and it asks by *counting over a sweep*, because a single
// sample of a shuffled order is indistinguishable from a lucky one.
array<int> $a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
int $i = 0;

// The sharpest form, and the one that separates this member from PHP's: over a
// two-entry sample the subject's order is one of two answers, so a member
// preserving it puts the lower-valued entry first *every* time. The subject's
// values ascend with their positions precisely so that `first < last` is the
// question "did this come back in subject order" spelled without a key. Both
// orders appear within 40 draws unless the order is not drawn at all — an
// implementation that preserves it gives 40, and one that reverses it gives 0,
// so this line is a bound on both sides rather than a hope about randomness.
int $ascending = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 40; $i = $i + 1) {
    var $two = Core\Random::sample($a, 2);
    $ascending = $ascending + (((Core\Arr::first($two) as int) < (Core\Arr::last($two) as int)) ? 1 : 0);
}
echo "pairs ", ($ascending > 0 && $ascending < 40) ? "both orders" : "one order", "\n";

// At full width a sample draws every entry, so it is a permutation and the
// same claim can be made against the whole subject at once: sorting the answer
// gives the subject back on every draw, while the answers themselves take more
// than one shape. That pair is what says "reordered" rather than "a different
// set" — the shape `shuffle` is swept for, asked of the member whose *count*
// happens to reach the whole array.
var $sorted = Core\Json::encode($a);
string $orders = "";
int $permutation = 0;
int $shapes = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 24; $i = $i + 1) {
    var $all = Core\Random::sample($a, 5);
    $permutation = $permutation + ((Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::sort($all)) == $sorted) ? 1 : 0);
    var $rendered = Core\Json::encode($all);
    $shapes = $shapes + (Core\Str::contains($orders, $rendered . ";") ? 0 : 1);
    $orders = $orders . $rendered . ";";
}
echo "full ", $permutation, " ", ($shapes > 1) ? "reordered" : "constant", "\n";

// The two widths where an order does not exist, where the member is still
// total: a zero-entry sample has nothing to order, and a one-entry sample has
// one arrangement. Neither is a refusal — R4's throw is for a count the
// subject cannot answer, which is the sibling case's last line.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Random::sample($a, 0)), "\n";
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Random::sample($a, 1)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
pairs both orders
full 24 reordered
[]
1
